  "body": "Ran across the alternate style sheet section of the HTML spec today, and afaict it seems to have regressed at some point between 4.01 and now? It's really hard to understand what it's trying to do, first of all. :) But secondly, it doesn't seem to handle the fact that adding a `title` attribute to `<link rel=stylesheet>` turns it into an alternate style even if the `alternate` keyword isn't set.\r\n\r\nThe only thing `alternate` does is, if set on a named set's stylesheet links, ensure that this link's title does not select the applied style set. It's `title` which creates mutually-exclusive sets, the first non-`alternate` one of which is applied by default.\r\n\r\nSee https://www.w3.org/TR/html4/present/styles.html#specifying-external",
